# The Smile
Tamil title: புன்னகை

**Friend:**

Siddhartha! Why do you look so distressed? What do you lack? You are the son of a renowned king. You have a delightful palace befitting an empire and a beautiful garden. Look there: a peahen clad in blue silk dances upon the green carpet, and a cuckoo sings. You have a virtuous wife possessing the peacock's grace, the cuckoo's sweet voice and a nature like the breeze, as well as a beloved child. With all this, why does your face droop? The moon has a blemish; you do not even have that. A rose has thorns, but your life is a thornless rose. Like melody joined to pitch, your life has every reason for enchantment and none for sorrow. The palace calls you and the crown awaits you. In the ceremonial hall, ministers and dignitaries wait eagerly for the day the prince ascends the throne. Life itself fondles and plays with you. Having obtained happiness that comes forward to embrace you, why are you troubled?

**Siddhartha:**

Friend, you have disturbed my thoughts. The charms you described welcome me with affection, and many people are ready to fill me with delight. Yet just as a dark cloud seizes and hides the moon, a sorrow torments the charm of my life. I have seen the moon drive away the cloud and emerge glittering. But for me, liberation from this anguish seems impossible.

**Friend:**

Prince, what causes your sorrow? Forgive me: are they the arrows of Manmatha...?

**Siddhartha:**

Shame! Before the shield of my wife's love, those arrows lose their points and fall to the ground. That is not what torments me.

**Friend:**

What other cause could there be?

**Siddhartha:**

Jasmine and fragrant *manoranjitham* flowers have been scattered upon the soft bed. The breeze blows, moonlight dances and the lute sounds. Yes, eager to enjoy all this, I approach the bed, but upon that flower-bed there is no maiden. There is a hissing cobra. I have seen the hissing cobra upon the bed of my life. Would that not disturb me? Tell me.

**Friend:**

Where is this hissing cobra?

**Siddhartha:**

Where? I see it everywhere. Upon the bed of life, across which comfort, pleasure, power and love crawl, lies a four-headed cobra named poverty, old age, disease and death. Having seen it, I no longer wish to lie upon life's bed.

**Friend:**

Is that all you meant? Very well! The moon that shines like milk wanes on some days. Darkness and light, pleasure and pain, are natural. Do we possess authority to change them? What path is there except to enjoy happiness when it comes and endure sorrow when it arrives?

**Siddhartha:**

I shall find a new path. Renouncing pleasures and leaving the kingdom, I shall wander and learn the art of untying the knot in which pleasure and pain are bound together. I want neither the adorned beauty of the women's quarters nor royal luxury. I shall engage in the work of understanding truth. I shall approach ascetics and listen attentively to their ideas. I shall crush the four-headed cobra.

Something like the dialogue imagined above may have occurred between Buddha and his friend. Before becoming an ascetic, the prince may have discussed these matters with a companion. If not, a conflict of such ideas must at least have taken place within his own mind. In the end Siddhartha took up the ascetic staff and became the Buddha.

With a gaze overflowing with compassion, he melted even the stone hearts of the impure. With a gentle smile he scorched the golden smiles of the priests. Through love he drove Aryan deceptions into retreat. Brahminism had imprisoned people by calling slaughter sacrifice, blindness scripture, obscene performances divine service and the curse of caste God's command. Buddha's revolution drove it into a corner. The Buddhist path defeated the merchants of deceit. But...

Buddhism, born in India, is no longer in India; it is in China. After Buddha's death, the Aryan serpent hidden in the undergrowth emerged and once again found work for its venomous fangs. Had Buddhism retained a place in India, would the condition of the country and its people have been what it is today?

The other day *Swadesamitran* wrote that the teachings of Lord Buddha, whom Mother India bore, constitute the religion followed by the Chinese today. When writing this sentence, the *Mitran*, which values the divine sports of Gopala Krishna, may have felt neither sorrow nor shame. It may instead have felt pride and elation. Perhaps its editor takes pride in the thought that our ancestors exiled the force of Buddha's revolution. But for us, ruined by Brahminism, reading it produces shame and grief. A fine red lotus bloomed in Aryan mud; it departed for the land of China. Where it bloomed, only Aryan mud remains once more. We forgot to follow the path shown by Buddhism. Shame! Sorrow!

Buddha did not merely renounce his kingdom. His path, like him, left India and went to China.

The *Mitran*, which now says, “The Chinese live by taking as their teaching the golden sayings given by Buddha, child of Mother India,” should explain why those golden sayings were rejected in the country of their birth.

Near Benares lies a place called Sarnath. The road is beautiful. Along much of it, a neem tree stands beside a mango tree. Siddhartha saw that bitterness and sweetness are intertwined in the world. As though illustrating this for us, neem and mango stand side by side on the road to the Buddha enclosure at Sarnath. As one approaches it, the neem trees disappear and only mango trees remain. People say Siddhartha travelled the road of life containing both pleasure and pain, attained the dawn of wisdom, removed suffering and found incomparable bliss. As though depicting this, the road near the Buddha enclosure contains only mango trees.

A bell donated by the Japanese Government hangs within the Buddha enclosure. Nearby, a wealthy Chinese man is constructing a new Buddhist shrine, where the Buddha image bears Chinese features. Buddha lived at Sarnath and delivered many teachings there. Yet today Sarnath is not the destination of millions of pilgrims; nearby Benares is regarded as the sacred centre.

At Sarnath, I saw the Buddha image smile as though saying, “Mother India bore me but did not care for me, so I went to another home,” and mockingly asking, “Have you no courage to question that injustice?” I wanted to tell Buddha, “You came from a royal family and became the Buddha, yet Brahminism tormented even you. What, then, will it do to me?”

Periyar E. V. Ramasamy was with me. Realising it was getting late, he struck his walking stick on the ground. Lost in thought, I awoke. The wind was blowing and that white beard was moving. I looked again at Buddha. I did not say aloud, “Brahminism has exiled you, but Aryanism has not completely extinguished the revolution you kindled. Look there: the foe of Brahminism!” I merely thought it in my mind about Periyar.

As though knowing even that thought, the Buddha of Sarnath seemed to look at me and say, “Good! Justice will stand and triumph. It will certainly triumph.”

That smile!
